userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add stable derived approval IDs and a detailed classified cache read without changing the legacy read's tested outputs.

**Motivation** — A standing grant that cannot be listed or revoked is not the grant D20 approved.

**Approach** — `approvalId(entry)` is the first 8 lowercase hex characters of the SHA-256 digest of `stage + "\0" + command + "\0" + approvedAt`, where a missing or non-string `approvedAt` is read as `""`. It is computed on every read and never stored. It is stable when other entries are added or removed; two entries recorded for the same (stage, command) at different times get different ids; two entries with an identical triple share an id and are removed together. `readApprovalsFileDetailed(path)` classifies the file: absent -> `missing`, `{ entries: [], taint: undefined }`; not JSON, or top level not a non-null non-array object -> `unparseable`, `{ entries: [], taint: undefined }`; top level an object whose `entries` is present and not an array -> `unparseable`, `{ entries: [], taint: parseTaint(taint) }` (the legacy read's result, so a present taint still withholds trust); otherwise -> `ok`, entries filtered by `isApprovalEntry`, taint parsed by `parseTaint` (both unchanged). `droppedMalformed` counts the array elements `isApprovalEntry` rejected (0 unless `ok`). `readApprovalsFile` keeps the same signature, implemented as `(await readApprovalsFileDetailed(path)).file`; its results are unchanged for every input. The reimplementation is behaviour-preserving: the existing `test/unit/permissions/approvals-store.test.ts` and `test/unit/permissions/approvals-link.test.ts` suites must stay green under `bun run test`.

**Scope** — In: store read and barrel exports; existing store tests. Out: locked removal (US-002), CLI listing (US-003/004).

**Interface**
```ts
export type ApprovalsFileState = "ok" | "missing" | "unparseable";
export interface ApprovalsFileRead {
  readonly file: ApprovalsFile;           // entries + taint, exactly what readApprovalsFile returns
  readonly state: ApprovalsFileState;
  readonly droppedMalformed: number;      // array elements isApprovalEntry rejected
}
export function approvalId(entry: ApprovalEntry): string;
export async function readApprovalsFileDetailed(path: string): Promise<ApprovalsFileRead>;
```

**Goal** — Add a locked, taint-preserving, read-decide-write removal primitive.

**Motivation** — Hand-editing `approvals.json` while a run appends to it can drop the taint marker that keeps the cache off after a forge-capable run.

**Approach** — `removeApprovals(path, decide)` runs entirely inside `withPathFileLock(path)`, in this order: (1) Read with `readApprovalsFileDetailed(path)`. (2) `state === "unparseable"` -> `{ outcome: "refused", reason: "approvals.json could not be parsed; not rewriting it" }`, no write. Rewriting would erase whatever the file holds. (3) `decide(read)` returns `{ refuse }` -> `{ outcome: "refused", reason: refuse }`, no write. (4) No entry selected (this includes a `missing` file) -> `{ outcome: "unchanged" }`, no write, no file or directory created. (5) Otherwise write `{ taint: read.file.taint, entries: <not selected> }` with `writeApprovalsFile` and return `{ outcome: "removed", removed, droppedMalformed }`. The taint marker is written back exactly as read. Nothing in this feature clears a taint; only a trusted run does, through `clearApprovalsTaint` (unchanged). Add one module docblock line naming `src/cli/approvals.ts` as the revocation surface D20 relies on.

**Scope** — In: store removal and its tests. Out: command-level selector validation (US-005), confirmation and error display (US-006), clearing or rewriting the approvals `taint` marker from the CLI.

**Interface**
```ts
export type RemovalDecision =
  | { readonly remove: (entry: ApprovalEntry) => boolean }
  | { readonly refuse: string };
export type RemovalResult =
  | { readonly outcome: "removed"; readonly removed: readonly ApprovalEntry[]; readonly droppedMalformed: number }
  | { readonly outcome: "unchanged" }
  | { readonly outcome: "refused"; readonly reason: string };
export async function removeApprovals(
  path: string,
  decide: (read: ApprovalsFileRead) => RemovalDecision,
): Promise<RemovalResult>;
```

**Goal** — Expose a human-readable approvals list through a registered Commander command.

**Motivation** — D20 justified no expiry by saying remembered approvals are auditable and revocable through `nax approvals list/rm`, which does not yet exist.

**Approach** — Deterministic file I/O and terminal output; no LLM, no network. The store module owns every read and write of `approvals.json`; the CLI module is terminal I/O only and reaches the store through the `src/permissions` barrel. Commands print raw: `list` shows each command byte-for-byte as stored. Store resolution copies `src/cli/runs.ts:14-19`: `loadConfig(workdir)` with a failure read as no config, project key `config?.name?.trim() || basename(workdir)`, then `projectOutputDir(key, config?.outputDir)` and `approvalsPath`. `bin/nax.ts` gains one import and one call, `registerApprovalsCommand(program)`, next to the `auth` group. Formatting helpers live in `src/cli/approvals-format.ts`. Register `nax approvals list [--json] [-d <dir>]`, with `-d/--dir` defaulting to the current directory. The registered action passes the command function's return value to `deps.exit`. For an `ok` store print `Approvals store: <path>`, trust line, `<n> remembered approvals`, blank line and each entry block. Entry line: `<id>  <stage>  <origin>  <approvedAt>  <approvedBy>  naxCommit <naxCommit>`, fields separated by two spaces. Then `          root <root>` (10 spaces), then the command's first line as `          $ <line>`, then each further command line prefixed by 12 spaces. Trust line: `Cache: trusted` or `Cache: TAINTED since <since> by run <runId> (pid <n>, alive) -- the cache is OFF; a trusted run will discard these entries.` with `exited` when dead and `(pid unknown)` without pid. Print the count for an empty `ok` store too. Add a `### nax approvals list` section to `docs/guides/cli-reference.md`. US-004 completes missing/unparseable/JSON behavior; US-005 adds rm.

**Scope** — In: registration, store resolution, human output, docs. Out: missing/unparseable and JSON body (US-004), removal (US-005/006), masking or redacting secrets in `nax approvals list` output.

**Interface**
```ts
export const _approvalsCliDeps: {
  readApprovalsFileDetailed: (path: string) => Promise<ApprovalsFileRead>;
  removeApprovals: typeof removeApprovals;
  isProcessAlive: (pid: number) => boolean;   // default: _approvalsTaintDeps.isProcessAlive
  confirm: (question: string) => Promise<boolean>; // default: promptForConfirmation
  isTTY: () => boolean;
  log: (text: string) => void;                // stdout
  logErr: (text: string) => void;             // stderr
  exit: (code: number) => void;               // default: process.exit
};
export async function resolveApprovalsFile(workdir: string): Promise<string>;
export async function approvalsListCommand(opts: { workdir: string; json: boolean }, deps?: typeof _approvalsCliDeps): Promise<number>;
export function registerApprovalsCommand(program: Command, deps?: typeof _approvalsCliDeps): void;
export function formatTrustLine(taint: ApprovalsTaint | undefined, isAlive: (pid: number) => boolean): string;
export function formatEntryBlock(entry: ApprovalEntry): readonly string[];
```

**Verification** — The `registerApprovalsCommand(program)` call in `bin/nax.ts` is verified by `bun run typecheck` plus a manual smoke: `bun bin/nax.ts approvals list -d <temp project>`.

**Goal** — Implement atomic CLI revocation by full entry ID or stage.

**Motivation** — A standing grant that cannot be revoked is not the grant D20 approved.

**Approach** — `rm` requires exactly one selector: one or more ids, `--stage <stage>`, or `--all`. Ids must match `^[0-9a-f]{8}$`; a malformed id is rejected before the store is read. Id removal is all-or-nothing: if any id matches no entry, nothing is removed. Use `removeApprovals` with the selector decision inside the lock; identical triples share an id and are removed together. Zero or several selectors produce stderr `Specify exactly one of <id...>, --stage <stage>, --all`, exit 1 without reading the store. Malformed id produces stderr `Invalid id: <id>`, exit 1 without reading. Unknown ids produce stderr `Unknown id(s): <ids>`, exit 1 without writing. Stage matches nothing: stdout `No entries for stage <stage>`, exit 0, no write. Each removed entry prints `removed <id>  <stage>  <preview>` on stdout, where `<preview>` is the command's first line cut to 80 characters. No confirmation for IDs or stage. Register `nax approvals rm <id...> [-d <dir>]` and `nax approvals rm --stage <stage> [-d <dir>]`; the registered action passes the command function's return value to `deps.exit`. US-006 finishes `--all` and store error mapping.

**Scope** — In: selector validation, locked ID/stage revocation, result formatting and registration. Out: `--all` prompts and error mapping (US-006), id-prefix matching, confirmation prompt for `rm <id...>` or `rm --stage`.

**Interface**
```ts
export async function approvalsRmCommand(
  opts: { workdir: string; ids: readonly string[]; stage?: string; all: boolean; yes: boolean },
  deps?: typeof _approvalsCliDeps,
): Promise<number>;
export function formatRemovedLine(entry: ApprovalEntry): string;             // `removed <id>  <stage>  <preview>`
export function registerApprovalsCommand(program: Command, deps?: typeof _approvalsCliDeps): void;
```
